        {
          "id": "9",
          "type": "proof",
          "question_latex": "\\text{If } u = \\ln(x^3+y^3+z^3-3xyz), \\text{ show } (\\partial_x+\\partial_y+\\partial_z)^2 u = \\frac{-9}{(x+y+z)^2}",
          "solution_steps": [
            "Use identity: $x^3+y^3+z^3-3xyz = (x+y+z)(x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx)$.",
            "Let $S=x+y+z$. Then $\\sum \\partial_x \\ln S = 3/S$.",
            "Applying operator again: $\\sum \\partial_x (3/S) = -9/S^2$."
          ],
          [cite_start]"source_ref": "Tut 1, Q9 [cite: 209]"
        }
